### Deletion of the Tail of a Linked List

**Problem:** Given the head of a singly linked list, delete the last node (tail) of the linked list and return the head of the modified linked list.

**Approach:**

- Check if the linked list is empty (`head == null`). If so, return `null`.
- If the linked list contains only one node (`head.next == null`), delete it by returning `null`.
- Initialize a temporary pointer with the head node.
- Traverse the linked list until reaching the second-last node (`temp.next.next == null`).
- Set the next reference of the second-last node to `null` (`temp.next = null`).
- This removes the last node from the linked list.
- Return the original head.

**Time Complexity:** O(n) – In the worst case, we traverse the linked list to reach the second-last node.

**Space Complexity:** O(1) – Only a temporary pointer is used.

**Concepts Used:**
- Singly Linked List
- Head Pointer
- Temporary Pointer
- Node Deletion
- Pointer Traversal
- Null Checking

###Solution

class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteTail(ListNode head) {
        // Edge case: If the list is empty or has only one node, return null
        if (head == null || head.next == null) {
            return null;
        }

        // Traverse to the second-to-last node
        ListNode current = head;
        while (current.next.next != null) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        // Disconnect the last node
        current.next = null;

        return head;
    }
}
